HO HO HO! Star-crossed lovers, versified with contempt:

They met, they swooned, they quoted stars, as if the cosmos kept their diary;
The stars in question could not care, being plasma, vast, and stationary.
Her family frowned, his family glowered, both households gifted them a map;
They chose instead a moonlit elopement — doomed, of course, by act and gap.
The moral, fools, is plainly writ: consult an atlas before kissing;
The heavens cross lovers daily, and nobody files the missing.

Poets are engineers of regret, and I am neither, mostly.
